// Draw the input as a water surface rather than bars.
//
// While there is sound, the middle of the surface rises and falls with the input level and sends
// waves toward both edges, and each syllable drops onto the surface at a varying place; the waves
// travel, overlap and die away, and in silence the surface settles to a flat line. The line is
// shaded with the water beneath it. The motion is simulated in the `SpeechState`, so liquid
// waveforms of one state and width show the same surface, and it starts from rest with each
// session. With reduced motion the surface holds a ripple in place that grows with the level.
//
// Waves need room to travel: use it for a waveform that spans a row, not a compact one beside a
// button.
//
// Liquid calls <gpui_kit::component::speech::SpeechWaveform>::liquid.
func (e SpeechWaveformEl) Liquid() SpeechWaveformEl {
	rt.Op(rt.N(e.Core), 256)
	return e
}

